lib/syntax/close_unclosed_doc_heredoc.ex:83 — the test that admits the input and the place the fix edits are two different lines. `def_below?`/`closing_quotes_below?` scan to end of file (a `-> false` branch in `Enum.find_value/3` means "keep looking", as lines 115-119 now correctly state), so the rule fires whenever there is a `def` *anywhere* below and no `"""` anywhere below — but `find_next_nonblank_offset/1` then inserts the closing `"""` before the **first non-blank line**. Whenever the unclosed doc actually has text in it (the shape the moduledoc itself describes: the LLM wrote the doc and forgot the closer), those differ and the terminator lands above the doc text instead of above the `def`. On `defmodule Sample do
  @doc """
  1 + 1

  def get(m, k), do: Map.get(m, k)
end
` the output is `@doc """` / `"""` / `1 + 1` / `def get…`: the doc is emptied and its content becomes live module-body code. That output *parses*, so neither guard can catch it — ProgressGuard sees tokenize_error → parse_error (rank up, always `:keep`), and the phase-level all-or-nothing gate only rolls back sources that still fail to parse. This is the exact failure mode the new comment on line 38-44 claims the ` ` prevents; the ` ` only narrows the no-`def`-at-all case, not this one. When the doc text does not parse (`Finds the min and max.`) the same rewrite is kept per-rule and then discarded with the whole round, so the rule silently repairs nothing on its commonest realistic input — and if another rule repairs the remaining error in the same round, the emptied doc commits. Pre-existing in shape (base `next_nonblank_is_def?` had the same whole-file scan), but this PR documents the whole-file scan as intended without reconciling the insertion point. Correct behaviour is to insert immediately before the line that matched `@def_line`, not before the first non-blank line.
